# IntraCore: параметры HAL блока из базы STM32CubeMX (db/mcu/IP/*_Modes.xml).
# Порождено tools/hal_import — руками не править. Формат: src/Docs/Description/HalCatalogFormat.md
[Ip]
Name=USBX
Version=v1.0.0_C0_Cube

[Parameters]
# имя|подпись|группа|единица|мин|макс|описание
UX_TRACE_INSERT_MACROS|UX_TRACE_INSERT_MACROS|TraceX||||Determine if tracing is enabled.
UX_DEVICE_BIDIRECTIONAL_ENDPOINT_SUPPORT|UX_DEVICE_BIDIRECTIONAL_ENDPOINT_SUPPORT|Device Core||||Enable device bi-directional endpoint support.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ACM_TRANSMISSION_DISABLE|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ACM_TRANSMISSION_DISABLE|Device Class CDC ACM||||disable CDC ACM non-blocking transmission support.
UX_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|UX_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|Core Utilities|Bytes|0|65535|This value is the size of the stack in bytes for the USBX threads.It can be typically 1024 or 2048 bytes depending on the processor used and the host controller.�
UX_HOST_ENUM_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|UX_HOST_ENUM_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|Host Core|Bytes|ThreadX:TX_MINIMUM_STACK|65535|Define USBX Host Enum Thread Stack Size
UX_HOST_HCD_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|UX_HOST_HCD_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|Host Core|Bytes|0|65535|Define USBX Host Thread Stack Size
UX_HOST_HNP_POLLING_THREAD_STACK|UX_HOST_HNP_POLLING_THREAD_STACK|Host Core|Bytes|0|65535|Define USBX Host HNP Polling Thread Stack Size
UX_MAX_HCD|UX_MAX_HCD|Host Core||||This value represents the number of different host controllers available in the system. For USB 1.1 support, this value will usually be 1. For USB 2.0 support, this value can be more than 1. This value represents the number of concurrent host controllers running at the same time. If for instance there are two instances of OHCI running, or one EHCI and one OHCI controller running, the UX_MAX_HCD should be set to 2
UX_MAX_HOST_LUN|UX_MAX_HOST_LUN|Host Class STORAGE||1|255|This value represents the maximum number of SCSI logical units represented in the host storage class driver
UX_PERIODIC_RATE|UX_PERIODIC_RATE|Core Utilities||0|65535|This value represents how many ticks per seconds for a specific hardware platform.
UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_MEMORY_BUFFER_SIZE|UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_MEMORY_BUFFER_SIZE|Host Class STORAGE||0|65535|This value represents the maximum number of bytes that a storage payload can send/receive.
UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_THREAD_STACK_SIZE|Host Class STORAGE|Bytes|0|65535|USBX Mass Storage Thread Stack Size
UX_MAX_ED|UX_MAX_ED|Host Controller||0|255|This value represents the maximum number of EDs in the controller pool. This number is assigned to one controller only. If multiple instances of controllers are present, this value is used by each individual controller
UX_MAX_TD|UX_MAX_TD|Host Controller||0|255|This value represents the maximum number of regular and isochronous TDs in the controller pool. This number is assigned to one controller only. If multiple instances of controllers are present, this value is used by each individual controller
UX_MAX_ISO_TD|UX_MAX_ISO_TD|Host Controller||0|255|This value represents the maximum number of regular and isochronous TDs in the controller pool. This number is assigned to one controller only. If multiple instances of controllers are present, this value is used by each individual controller
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_DECOMPRESSION_BUFFER|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_DECOMPRESSION_BUFFER|Host HID Core||0|65535|This value represents the maximum size of the HID decompressed buffer. This cannot be determined in advance so we allocate a big block, usually 4K but for simple HID devices like keyboard and mouse it can be reduced a lot
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_USAGES|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_USAGES|Host HID Core||0|65535|This value represents the maximum number of HID usages for a HID device
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_INTERRUPT_OUT_SUPPORT|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_INTERRUPT_OUT_SUPPORT|Host HID Core||||Add support for Host HID OUT interrupt
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_REPORT_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_REPORT_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT|Host HID Core||0|65535|Define HID report transfer timeout value in millisecond. The default is 10000 milliseconds.
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE|Host Class HID Keyboard||||If defined, this value causes ux_host_class_hid_keyboard_key_get to only report key changes i.e. key presses and key releases. By default, it only reports when a key is down.
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE_REPORT_KEY_DOWN_ONLY|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE_REPORT_KEY_DOWN_ONLY|Host Class HID Keyboard||||This value causes ux_host_class_hid_keyboard_key_get to only report key changes i.e. key presses and key releases. By default, it only reports when a key is down.
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE_REPORT_LOCK_KEYS|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE_REPORT_LOCK_KEYS|Host Class HID Keyboard||||This value causes ux_host_class_hid_keyboard_key_get to report lock key (CapsLock/NumLock/ScrollLock) changes.
UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE_REPORT_MODIFIER_KEYS|UX_HOST_CLASS_HID_KEYBOARD_EVENTS_KEY_CHANGES_MODE_REPORT_MODIFIER_KEYS|Host Class HID Keyboard||||This value causes ux_host_class_hid_keyboard_key_get to report modifier key (Ctrl/Alt/Shift/GUI) changes.
UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_MAX_MEDIA|UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_MAX_MEDIA|Host Class STORAGE||1|8|This value represents the maximum number of media for the host storage class.
UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_INCLUDE_LEGACY_PROTOCOL_SUPPORT|UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_INCLUDE_LEGACY_PROTOCOL_SUPPORT|Host Class STORAGE||||This value includes code to handle storage devices that use the CB or CBI protocol (such as floppy disks).
UX_ENFORCE_SAFE_ALIGNMENT|UX_ENFORCE_SAFE_ALIGNMENT|Core Utilities||||This value forces the memory allocation scheme to enforce alignement of memory with the UX_SAFE_ALIGN field.
UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_NX_PKPOOL_ENTRIES|UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_NX_PKPOOL_ENTRIES|Host Class CDC ECM||0|65535|This value represents the number of packets in the CDC_ECM host class.
UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_PACKET_POOL_INSTANCE_WAIT|UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_PACKET_POOL_INSTANCE_WAIT|Host Class CDC ECM||0|65535|This value represents the number of milliseconds to wait for packet instance allocation until invoking the application's error callback and retrying.
UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_USE_PACKET_POOL_FROM_NETX|UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_USE_PACKET_POOL_FROM_NETX|Host Class CDC ECM||||Defined, this enables CDC ECM class to use the packet pool from NetX instance.
UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_PACKET_POOL_WAIT|UX_HOST_CLASS_CDC_ECM_PACKET_POOL_WAIT|Host Class CDC ECM||0|65535|This value represents the number of milliseconds to wait for packet allocation until invoking the application's error callback and retrying.
UX_HOST_SIDE_ONLY|UX_HOST_SIDE_ONLY|Host Core||||This value will only enable the host side of usbx.
UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_MAX_TRANSFER_SIZE|UX_HOST_CLASS_STORAGE_MAX_TRANSFER_SIZE|Host Class STORAGE||0|65535|This value represents the maximum size of single tansfers for the SCSI data phase.
UX_DEBUG_LOG_SIZE|UX_DEBUG_LOG_SIZE|Core Utilities||0|65535|This value represents the size of the log pool.
UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_ENUM|UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_ENUM|Core Utilities||0|255|This is the ThreadX priority value for the USBX enumeration threads that monitors the bus topology
UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_CLASS|UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_CLASS|Core Utilities||0|255|This is the ThreadX priority value for the standard USBX threads
UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_KEYBOARD|UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_KEYBOARD|Host Class HID Keyboard||0|255|
UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_HCD|UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_HCD|Host Core||0|255|This is the ThreadX priority value for the host controller thread.
UX_NO_TIME_SLICE|UX_NO_TIME_SLICE|Core Utilities||0|255|This value actually defines the time slice that will be used for threads. For example, if defined to 0, the ThreadX target port does not use time slices.
UX_ENABLE_ERROR_CHECKING|UX_ENABLE_ERROR_CHECKING|Core Utilities||||This option enables the basic USBX error checking. This define is typically used when the application is debugging and removed after the application is fully debugged.
UX_ALIGN_MIN|UX_ALIGN_MIN|Core Utilities||||Define the minimal allocated memory alignment in number of bytes: - Align 8. - Align 16. - Align 32. - Align 64. - Align 128. - Align 256. - Align 512. - Align 1024. - Align 2048. - Align 4096. - Safe Align. - Max Align.
UX_NAME_REFERENCED_BY_POINTER|UX_NAME_REFERENCED_BY_POINTER|Core Utilities||||Define the _name in structs are referenced by pointer instead of by contents.
UX_MAX_SLAVE_CLASS_DRIVER|UX_MAX_SLAVE_CLASS_DRIVER|Device Core||0|255|This value is the maximum number of classes in the device stack that can be loaded by USBX
UX_MAX_SLAVE_INTERFACES|UX_MAX_SLAVE_INTERFACES|Device Core||0|255|This value is the maximum number of interfaces in the device framework
UX_MAX_DEVICES|UX_MAX_DEVICES|Host Core||1|127|This value represents the maximum number of devices that can be attached to the USB. Normally, the theoretical maximum number on a single USB is 127 devices. This value can be scaled down to conserve memory. Note that this value represents the total number of devices regardless of the number of USB buses in the system.
UX_MAX_SLAVE_LUN|UX_MAX_SLAVE_LUN|Device Core||1|255|This value represents the current number of SCSI logical units represented in the device storage class driver.
UX_SLAVE_REQUEST_CONTROL_MAX_LENGTH|UX_SLAVE_REQUEST_CONTROL_MAX_LENGTH|Device Core||64|65535|This value represents the maximum number of bytes received on a control endpoint in the device stack
UX_SLAVE_REQUEST_DATA_MAX_LENGTH|UX_SLAVE_REQUEST_DATA_MAX_LENGTH|Device Core||64|65535|This value represents the maximum number of bytes that can be received or transmitted on any endpoint. This value cannot be less than the maximum packet size of any endpoint.
UX_DEVICE_ALTERNATE_SETTING_SUPPORT_DISABLE|UX_DEVICE_ALTERNATE_SETTING_SUPPORT_DISABLE|Device Core||||It disables interface alternate setting support.
UX_DEVICE_INITIALIZE_FRAMEWORK_SCAN_DISABLE|UX_DEVICE_INITIALIZE_FRAMEWORK_SCAN_DISABLE|Device Core||||It disables device framework scan, where max number of endpoints (except EP0) and max number of interfaces are calculated at runtime, as a base to allocate memory for interfaces and endpoints structures and their buffers.
UX_SLAVE_CLASS_STORAGE_INCLUDE_MMC|UX_SLAVE_CLASS_STORAGE_INCLUDE_MMC|Device Class STORAGE||||this value includes code to handle storage Multi-Media Commands (MMC). E.g., DVD-ROM.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_NX_PKPOOL_ENTRIES|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_NX_PKPOOL_ENTRIES|Device Class CDC ECM||0|65535|This value represents the number of packets in the CDC_ECM device class.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_ZERO_COPY|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_ZERO_COPY|Device Class CDC ECM||||Defined, it enables device CDC_ECM zero copy support (works if CDC_ECM owns endpoint buffer). Enabled, it requires that the NX IP default packet pool is in cache safe area, and buffer max size is larger than UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_ETHERNET_PACKET_SIZE (1536).
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_PACKET_POOL_WAIT|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ECM_PACKET_POOL_WAIT|Device Class CDC ECM||0|65535|This value represents the number of milliseconds to wait for packet allocation until invoking the application's error callback and retrying
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_EVENT_BUFFER_LENGTH|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_EVENT_BUFFER_LENGTH|Device Class HID||0|1024|This value represents the the maximum length of HID reports on the device.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_MAX_EVENTS_QUEUE|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_MAX_EVENTS_QUEUE|Device Class HID||0|255|This value represents the the maximum number of HID events/reports that can be queued at once.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_ZERO_COPY|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_ZERO_COPY|Device Class HID||||Defined, it enables device HID zero copy and flexible queue support (works if HID owns endpoint buffer). Enabled, the internal queue buffer is directly used for transfer, the APIs are kept to keep backword compatibility, to AVOID KEEPING BUFFERS IN APPLICATION. Flexible queue introduces initialization parameter _event_max_number and _event_max_length, so each HID function could have different queue settings._event_max_number could be 2 ~UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_MAX_EVENTS_QUEUE.Max of _event_max_length could be UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_EVENT_BUFFER_LENGTH. If the initialization parameters are invalid (are 0s or exceed upper mentioned definition), UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_MAX_EVENTS_QUEUE and UX_DEVICE_CLASS_HID_EVENT_BUFFER_LENGTH are used to calculate and allocate the queue.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_AUDIO_INTERRUPT_SUPPORT|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_AUDIO_INTERRUPT_SUPPORT|Device Class AUDIO||||Enable the device audio interrupt endpoint support.
UX_HOST_CLASS_AUDIO_2_SUPPORT|UX_HOST_CLASS_AUDIO_2_SUPPORT|Host Class AUDIO||||Enable the host audio UAC 2.0 supported.
UX_HOST_CLASS_AUDIO_FEEDBACK_SUPPORT|UX_HOST_CLASS_AUDIO_FEEDBACK_SUPPORT|Host Class AUDIO||||Enable the host audio Feedback support.
UX_HOST_CLASS_AUDIO_INTERRUPT_SUPPORT|UX_HOST_CLASS_AUDIO_INTERRUPT_SUPPORT|Host Class AUDIO||||Enable the device audio interrupt endpoint support.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_AUDIO_FEEDBACK_SUPPORT|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_AUDIO_FEEDBACK_SUPPORT|Device Class AUDIO||||Enable the device audio Feedback support.
UX_HOST_DEVICE_CLASS_CODE_VALIDATION_ENABLE|UX_HOST_DEVICE_CLASS_CODE_VALIDATION_ENABLE|Core Utilities||||Enables host device class code validation
UX_CONTROL_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT|UX_CONTROL_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT|Core Utilities||0|65535|Define control transfer timeout value in millisecond. The default is 10000 milliseconds.
UX_NON_CONTROL_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT|UX_NON_CONTROL_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT|Core Utilities||0|65535|Define non control transfer timeout value in millisecond. The default is 50000 milliseconds.
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ACM_WRITE_AUTO_ZLP|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_CDC_ACM_WRITE_AUTO_ZLP|Device Class CDC ACM||||Enable automatic pending ZLP (complete transfer) after buffer is sent.
UX_DEVICE_SIDE_ONLY|UX_DEVICE_SIDE_ONLY|Device Core||||This value will only enable the device side of usbx
UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_DCD|UX_THREAD_PRIORITY_DCD|Device Core||0|255|This is the ThreadX priority value for the device controller thread.
UX_MAX_CLASS_DRIVER|UX_MAX_CLASS_DRIVER|Host Core||0|255|This value is the maximum number of classes that can be loaded by USBX. This value represents the class container and not the number of instances of a class.
UX_MAX_DEVICE_ENDPOINTS|UX_MAX_DEVICE_ENDPOINTS|Device Core||0|255|It defines USBX device max number of endpoints (1~n). <font color="red"> It must be greater or equal than the numbre of endpoints used. </font>
UX_MAX_DEVICE_INTERFACES|UX_MAX_DEVICE_INTERFACES|Device Core||0|255|it define USBX device max number of interfacess (1~n).
UX_MAX_ROOTHUB_PORT|UX_MAX_ROOTHUB_PORT|Host Class Hub||1|255|Define USBX max root hub port (1 ~ n).
UX_MAX_TT|UX_MAX_TT|Host Core||1|255|Define USBX max TT.
USBX_Version|USBX version|Version||||USBX version
UX_PIMA_WITH_MTP_SUPPORT|UX_PIMA_WITH_MTP_SUPPORT|Device Class PIMA||||Enables device/host PIMA MTP support
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_CMD_HS_BINTERVAL|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_HS_BINTERVAL|Device Class PIMA||1|255|Device PIMA class endpoint cmd in HS BINERVAL
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_CMD_ADDR|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_ADDR|Device Class PIMA||1|9|Device PIMA class command endpoint in Address, should have a unique value when used alongside other device classes
USBD_PIMA_EP_OUT_ADDR|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_OUT_ADDR|Device Class PIMA||1|9|Device PIMA endpoint out address, should have a unique address when used alongside other device classes.
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_FS_MPS|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_FS_MPS|Device Class PIMA||1|64|Device PIMA class endpoint in FS max packet size
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_CMD_FS_MPS|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_FS_MPS|Device Class PIMA||1|64|Device PIMA class endpoint in FS max packet size
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_HS_MPS|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_HS_MPS|Device Class PIMA||1|512|Device PIMA class endpoint in HS max packet size
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_CMD_FS_BINTERVAL|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_FS_BINTERVAL|Device Class PIMA||1|255|Device PIMA class endpoint cmd in FS BINERVAL
USBD_PIMA_EP_OUT_FS_MPS|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_OUT_FS_MPS|Device Class PIMA||1|64|Device PIMA class endpoint out FS max packet size
USBD_PIMA_EP_OUT_HS_MPS|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_OUT_HS_MPS|Device Class PIMA||1|512|Device PIMA class endpoint out HS max packet size
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_ADDR|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_ADDR|Device Class PIMA||1|9|Device PIMA endpoint in address, should have a unique address when used alongside other device classes.
USBD_PIMA_EP_IN_CMD_HS_MPS|USBD_PIMA_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_HS_MPS|Device Class PIMA||1|512|Device PIMA class endpoint HS max packet size
USBD_RNDIS_EPINCMD_FS_MPS|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_FS_MPS|Device Class RNDIS||1|64|Device RNDIS class endpoint command FS max packet size.
USBD_RNDIS_EPIN_ADDR|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_ADDR|Device Class RNDIS||1|9|When UX_DEVICE_BIDIRECTIONAL_ENDPOINT_SUPPORT disabled, device RNDIS endpoint in address, should have a unique address when used alongside other device classes.
USBD_RNDIS_LOCAL_MAC_ADDR|USBD_RNDIS_LOCAL_MAC_ADDR|Device Class RNDIS||||Device RNDIS MAC address. Make sure to use a valid local MAC address.
USBD_RNDIS_EPIN_HS_MPS|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_HS_MPS|Device Class RNDIS||1|512|Device RNDIS class endpoint in HS max packet size.
USBD_RNDIS_REMOTE_MAC_ADDR|USBD_RNDIS_REMOTE_MAC_ADDR|Device Class RNDIS||||Device RNDIS MAC address. Make sure to use a valid remote MAC address.
USBD_RNDIS_EPINCMD_FS_BINTERVAL|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_FS_BINTERVAL|Device Class RNDIS||1|16|Device RNDIS class endpoint command in FS BINERVAL.
USBD_RNDIS_EPOUT_ADDR|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_OUT_ADDR|Device Class RNDIS||1|9|When UX_DEVICE_BIDIRECTIONAL_ENDPOINT_SUPPORT disabled, device RNDIS endpoint out address, should have a unique address when used alongside other device classes.
USBD_RNDIS_EPINCMD_ADDR|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_ADDR|Device Class RNDIS||1|9|When UX_DEVICE_BIDIRECTIONAL_ENDPOINT_SUPPORT disabled, device RNDIS class command endpoint in Address, should have a unique value when used alongside other device classes .
USBD_RNDIS_EPOUT_FS_MPS|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_OUT_FS_MPS|Device Class RNDIS||1|64|Device RNDIS class endpoint out FS max packet size.
USBD_RNDIS_EPOUT_HS_MPS|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_OUT_HS_MPS|Device Class RNDIS||1|512|Device RNDIS class endpoint out HS max packet size.
USBD_RNDIS_EPINCMD_HS_BINTERVAL|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_HS_BINTERVAL|Device Class RNDIS||1|16|Device RNDIS class endpoint command in HS BINERVAL
USBD_RNDIS_EPINCMD_HS_MPS|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_CMD_HS_MPS|Device Class RNDIS||1|512|Device RNDIS class endpoint command HS max packet size.
USBD_RNDIS_EPIN_FS_MPS|USBD_RNDIS_ENDPOINT_IN_FS_MPS|Device Class RNDIS||1|64|Device RNDIS class endpoint in FS max packet size
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_RNDIS_ZERO_COPY|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_RNDIS_ZERO_COPY|Device Class RNDIS||||Defined, it enables device RNDIS zero copy support (works if RNDIS owns endpoint buffer). Enabled, it requires that the NX IP default packet pool is in cache safe area, and buffer max size is larger than UX_DEVICE_CLASS_RNDIS_MAX_PACKET_TRANSFER_SIZE (1600).
USBD_MSC_EPIN_ADDR|USBD_MSC_ENDPOINT_IN_ADDR|Device Class STORAGE||0|9|this the device storage endpoint in address, <font color="red">make sure to have a unique address for the same class and alongside other device classes.</font>
USBD_MSC_EPOUT_ADDR|USBD_MSC_ENDPOINT_OUT_ADDR|Device Class STORAGE||0|9|this the device storage endpoint out address, <font color="red">make sure to have a unique address for the same class and alongside other device classes.</font>
USBD_HID_EPIN_ADDR|USBD_HID_ENDPOINT_IN_ADDRESS|Device Class HID||0|9|this the device HID endpoint in address, <font color="red">make sure to have a unique address when used alongside other device classes.</font>
USBD_MSC_EPOUT_FS_MPS|USBD_MSC_ENDPOINT_OUT_FS_MPS|Device Class STORAGE||||Device storage class endpoint out_FS Max Packet Size
USBD_DFU_XFER_SIZE|USBD_DFU_XFER_SIZE|Device Class DFU||0|16777215|Maximum number of bytes that the device can accept per control-write operation. Note : The value of UX_SLAVE_REQUEST_CONTROL_MAX_LENGTH shall be greater or equal to USBD_DFU_XFER_SIZE
USBD_DFU_MEDIA|USBD_DFU_MEDIA|Device Class DFU||||USBD_DFU_MEDIA Parameter Description: The description of the flash (used by PC tool DFuSe) Each Alternate setting string descriptor must follow this memory mapping so that the PC Host Software can decode the right mapping for the selected device: ● @: To detect that this is a special mapping descriptor (to avoid decoding standard descriptor) ● /: for separator between zones ● Maximum 8 digits per address starting by “0x” ● /: for separator between zones ● Maximum of 2 digits for the number of sectors ● *: For separator between number of sectors and sector size ● Maximum 3 digits for sector size between 0 and 999 ● 1 digit for the sector size multiplier. Valid entries are: B (byte), K (Kilo), M (Mega) ● 1 digit for the sector type as follows: – a (0x41): Readable – b (0x42): Erasable – c (0x43): Readable and Erasabled - d(0x44): Writeable – e (0x45): Readable and Writeable – f (0x46): Erasable and Writeable – g (0x47): Readable, Erasable and Writeable Note: If the target memory is not contiguous, the user can add the new sectors to be decoded just after a slash"/" as shown in the following example: "@Flash /0xF000/1*4Ka/0xE000/1*4Kg/0x8000/2*24Kg"
USBD_DFU_DetachTimeout|USBD_DFU_DetachTimeout|Device Class DFU||0|16777215|Time, in milliseconds, that the device will wait after receipt of the DFU_DETACH request. If this time elapses without a USB reset, then the device will terminate the Reconfiguration phase and revert back to normal operation. This represents the maximum time that the device can wait (depending on its timers, etc.).
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_STATUS_POLLTIMEOUT|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_STATUS_POLLTIMEOUT|Device Class DFU||0|16777215|This value represents the default DFU status bwPollTimeout. The value is 3 bytes long (max 0xFFFFFFu). By default the bwPollTimeout is 1 (means 1ms)
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_CUSTOM_REQUEST_ENABLE|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_CUSTOM_REQUEST_ENABLE|Device Class DFU||||This macro will enable custom request process callback
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_ERROR_GET_ENABLE|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_ERROR_GET_ENABLE|Device Class DFU||||This macro will enable DFU_GETSTATUS and DFU_GETSTATE in dfu ERROR
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_UPLOAD_DISABLE|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_UPLOAD_DISABLE|Device Class DFU||||This macro disable DFU Upload support
USBD_DFU_BM_ATTRIBUTES|USBD_DFU_BM_ATTRIBUTES|Device Class DFU||0|255|Bit 3: device will perform a bus detach-attach sequence when it receives a DFU_DETACH request. The host must not issue a USB Reset. (bitWillDetach) 0 = no 1 = yes Bit 2: device is able to communicate via USB after Manifestation phase. (bitManifestationTolerant) 0 = no, must see bus reset 1 = yes Bit 1: upload capable (bitCanUpload) 0 = no 1 = yes Bit 0: download capable (bitCanDnload) 0 = no 1 = yes
MEDIA_ERASE_TIME|DFU_MEDIA_ERASE_TIME|Device Class DFU||0|16777215|Time to erase media 1 (means 1ms)
UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_STATUS_MODE|UX_DEVICE_CLASS_DFU_STATUS_MODE|Device Class DFU||||simple mode enabled: status is queried from application in dfuDNLOAD-SYNC and dfuMANIFEST-SYNC state, (no bwPollTimeout.) simple mode disabled: status is queried from application once requested, (bwPollTimeout supported)
MEDIA_PROGRAM_TIME|DFU_MEDIA_PROGRAM_TIME|Device Class DFU||0|16777215|time to media prgram 1 (means 1ms)
USBD_MSC_EPIN_HS_MPS|USBD_MSC_ENDPOINT_IN_HS_MPS|Device Class STORAGE||||Device storage class endpoint in_HS Max Packet Size
USBD_MSC_EPOUT_HS_MPS|USBD_MSC_ENDPOINT_OUT_HS_MPS|Device Class STORAGE||||Device storage class endpoint out_HS Max Packet Size
USBD_MSC_EPIN_FS_MPS|USBD_MSC_ENDPOINT_IN_FS_MPS|Device Class STORAGE||||Device storage class endpoint in_FS Max Packet Size
USBD_HID_MOUSE_EPIN_ADDR|USBD_HID_MOUSE_ENDPOINT_IN_ADDRESS|Device Class HID MOUSE||0|9|this the device HID endpoint in address, <font color="red">make sure to have a unique address when used alongside other device classes.</font>
USBD_HID_MOUSE_EPIN_HS_MPS|USBD_HID_MOUSE_ENDPOINT_IN_HS_MPS|Device Class HID MOUSE||1|1024|Device HID class endpoint in_HS Max Packet Size
